# Line endings belong to the REPO, not to each machine's git config.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, stated precisely, because the obvious reason turned out to be wrong.
#
# Every text blob in this repo is already stored as pure LF — 1,551 of 1,551 verified, zero CRLF.
# `core.autocrlf=true` on the Windows box hands you CRLF on checkout and converts back to LF on
# commit, which is exactly what it is for. So:
#
#   * scripts/deploy.sh is LF IN THE INDEX. A Linux or container checkout gets LF and runs fine.
#     The `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M` failure is real as a mechanism but cannot happen
#     from this repo's committed state — the CRLF exists only in the Windows working copy.
#   * the ~3,000-line phantom diff is likewise already prevented on this box, by the same setting.
#
# THE ACTUAL RISK IS THAT core.autocrlf IS A PER-MACHINE SETTING AND NOTHING ENFORCES IT. A clone
# on a machine where it is unset — another Windows box, a CI runner, a container, a new session —
# would commit CRLF the first time someone edits a file there. THEN the shebang breaks on Linux and
# a 60-line change reports as 3,000. Today the convention is a property of one laptop's config;
# this file makes it a property of the repository, which is the difference between "it happens to
# be right" and "it cannot go wrong".
#
# ⚠ ADDING THIS CHANGES NO STORED BYTES. Verified before committing, in a throwaway clone with
# `git add --renormalize .`: ZERO blobs differ. That check matters — on a repo that HAD stored
# CRLF, this same file would rewrite every one of them at once and produce exactly the unreviewable
# diff it is meant to prevent. Run that check before adding a .gitattributes anywhere else.

# Normalise to LF in the index; check out in the platform's own convention.
* text=auto

# Executables where the line ending is not cosmetic: a trailing \r joins the interpreter's NAME.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Declared rather than left to git's NUL-byte heuristic. A file that is mostly text with one
# embedded NUL is guessed as binary; a file with none is guessed as text and gets rewritten.
# Guessing is fine until the guess is wrong, and a corrupted font or image is not obvious in review.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.db   binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
